To extract a tar file in Windows Terminal (using Command Prompt or PowerShell), you can use the built-in tar command introduced in Windows 10. [1, 2] 
## Basic Extraction Command
The most common command to extract a .tar or .tar.gz file into your current directory is: [3, 4] 

tar -xvzf filename.tar.gz

## Breakdown of Command Flags

* -x: Tells tar to extract the files.
* -v: Enables verbose mode, showing you the list of files as they are being extracted.
* -z: Tells tar to decompress the file (required for .gz or .tgz formats).
* -f: Informs the utility that the next argument is the filename. [5, 6, 7, 8] 

## Advanced Options

* Extract to a specific folder: Use the -C (uppercase) flag followed by the destination path.

tar -xvzf filename.tar.gz -C C:\Path\To\Destination

* Extract a simple .tar (not compressed): You can omit the -z flag.

tar -xvf filename.tar

[6, 7, 9, 10] 

## Step-by-Step Instructions

   1. Open Windows Terminal: Press Win + X and select Terminal or PowerShell.
   2. Navigate to your file: Use the cd command to enter the folder where your file is saved.
   * Tip: You can right-click the file in File Explorer and select Copy as path to quickly get the full directory.
   3. Run the command: Type the extraction command and press Enter. [11, 12, 13] 


To extract a ZIP file in Windows Terminal, you can use either the tar command or a PowerShell cmdlet. [1, 2, 3] 
## Option 1: Using the tar Command
This is the simplest way and works in both Command Prompt and PowerShell on Windows 10 (build 17063+) and Windows 11. [2, 4] 

* Extract to current folder:

tar -xf filename.zip

* Extract to a specific folder:

tar -xf filename.zip -C C:\Path\To\Extract

[2, 5, 6] 

## Option 2: Using PowerShell (Expand-Archive) [7] 
If you are already in a PowerShell tab, you can use a native cmdlet designed specifically for ZIP files. [3, 8] 

* Basic extraction:

Expand-Archive -Path "C:\path\to\file.zip" -DestinationPath "C:\destination\folder"

Compress-Archive -Path "C:\path\to\file.zip" -DestinationPath "C:\destination\folder"

* Overwrite existing files: Add the -Force parameter to the end if the destination folder already contains files with the same names. [2, 3] 

## Using a Third-Party Tool (7-Zip) [9] 
If you have 7-Zip installed, you can use its command-line version (7z.exe) for more advanced options or better compression. [10, 11] 

7z x filename.zip -o"C:\destination\folder"

Pro Tip: If your file path has spaces, always wrap it in double quotes (e.g., "C:\My Folder\file.zip") to avoid errors. [3] 

